Sparse Matrix-Vector Multiplication (SpMV) is a core kernel in high-performance computing (HPC) and artificial intelligence (AI) applications. However, in multi-task scheduling scenarios within heterogeneous computing centers, its performance is often difficult to predict due to the complexity of matrix sparsity structures. Existing performance prediction methods face a dilemma, traditional statistical models are computationally efficient but fail to capture micro-texture features, leading to distorted predictions for long-tail samples; conversely, deep learning models offer high accuracy but incur significant inference overhead, making them unsuitable for the real-time requirements of online scheduling. To address this challenge, this paper proposes ViSim, a lightweight offline performance simulation framework based on residual learning. The framework adopts a decoupled dual-stream architecture, utilizing low-dimensional statistical features to quickly anchor a performance baseline, while leveraging a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to extract density map texture features of the matrix. Through an additive residual mechanism, ViSim specifically corrects performance errors that the statistical model cannot explain. Experiments on NVIDIA V100, Hygon Z100, and Hygon BW1000 GPUs demonstrate that ViSim achieves extremely high prediction accuracy (R2 > 0.98) while maintaining low inference overhead, accounting for less than 1% of the execution time in large-scale matrix computations. This exhibits excellent cross-platform generalization capability and engineering practicality.